<h1><span lang="EN-GB">New Line - Non-Interactive</span></h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-US">This command is accessible from</span> <i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Datuming &gt; Non-Interactive &gt;</b> <i style="font-style:normal; font-weight:bold;">Line</i></span></i></p>
<p class="description"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b><i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>D</b></span></i>escription:</b></span> <span class="Command">New Line</span> <span lang="EN-US">command creates new datum line.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify; font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Image004.gif" width="449px" height="381px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span><span lang="EN-GB"><i style="font-style:normal;"><span lang="EN-US">C</span></i></span></span>ommon parts of the dialog</p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Name</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– name for the line. The default name is ‘Line N’, where N is the number of the subsequent newly created datum line.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><b style="font-weight:bold;"><span lang="EN-GB">Result</span>: When all necessary entities are selected from the lists, the result, which contains the co-ordinates of the first point, co-ordinates of the vector of direction, and the length of the line, is displayed:</b></p>
<p><i style="font-style:normal;"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image014.jpg" width="448px" height="156px" border="0" class="img_whs5" alt=""></span></i></p>
<h2 class="whs6"><i><span lang="EN-US" style="font-style:normal;">M</span></i><i style="font-style:normal;"><span lang="EN-US">ethods:</span></i></h2>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Co-ordinates</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image006.jpg" width="402px" height="106px" border="0" class="img_whs7" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– co-ordinates of the first point on the line.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– co-ordinates of the second point on line.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">Point 1 and Point 2 should not have to coincide.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Two Points</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image008.jpg" width="407px" height="92px" border="0" class="img_whs8" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list to define the first point on the line.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list to define the second point on the line.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">Point 1 and Point 2 must be two different points and should not have to coincide. In case of sphere and circle, their centres are used as points.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Two Planes</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Image003.gif" width="408px" height="96px" border="0" class="img_whs9" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB">The line is defined as the intersection of the two planes.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">Plane 1 and Plane 2 must be two different planes and should not have to be parallel.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Cylinder</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Line_-_Cylinder.GIF" width="420px" height="124px" border="0" class="img_whs10" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>From list</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– creates a line from the axis of the cylinder selected from the list.</span></p>
<ul type="disc" class="whs11">
<li style="text-align:justify; list-style:circle;" type="circle" class="kadov-p">
<p><span lang="EN-GB">Cylinder – select an existing cylinder from the list.</span></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>From selection</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– creates multiple lines from the axes of all visible and selected cylinders.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Points and Offsets</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Line_-_Points_and_Offsets.GIF" width="417px" height="123px" border="0" class="img_whs12" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Offset 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– type-in the positive or negative offset in mm for Point 1.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Offset 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– type-in the positive or negative offset in mm for Point 2.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify; font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">and</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">are projected onto the plane. The offsets, that are orthogonal to the line connecting the projections, are the end-points of the newly created line. Orientation of the offsets depends on the order of the selected points and follows the right-hand rule. There are two possible solutions. To get the second solution, simply select the points in the reverse order.</span></p>
<p><img src="Using_Datums_files/Line_-_Points_and_Offsets_-_scheme.GIF" width="447px" height="332px" border="0" class="img_whs13" alt=""></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Projection</span></p>
<p class="whs14"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-US"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Line_-_Projection.GIF" width="416px" height="121px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></span></span></b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">The line is defined as the projection of the selected line onto the plane. In case of cylinder, its axis is used for projection.</font></span></p>
<p class="whs16">&#160;</p>
